For \code{wildcard_rules_not_inherited}, a crawler obeys only the groups that
name it (combined when several do) and ignores the \verb{User-agent: *} group,
as RFC 9309 section 2.2.1 says. Each \code{*} Allow or Disallow is compared
with the named crawler's rules by type and value; a trailing \code{*} is
ignored (\verb{/x} equals \verb{/x*}, and \code{*} and \verb{/*} equal \code{/}), \code{$} is not, and
empty values are left out. The message lists the missing \code{*} rules, ten at
most. A crawler whose rules are all explicit allow-all (an empty
\verb{Disallow:}, \verb{Allow: /}, \verb{Allow: /*} or \verb{Allow: *}) is not reported; an
empty \verb{Allow:} is ignored, and a crawler whose groups have no other rule
is reported.

For \code{disallow_tied_by_allow}, an Allow and a Disallow with the same
pattern match the same paths with the same priority, and Allow wins the
tie (RFC 9309 section 2.2.2), so the Disallow never applies; with
\verb{Allow: /}, a \verb{Disallow: /} blocks nothing. The comparison runs on the
Google parser's canonical form of each value (\verb{\%aa} equals \verb{\%AA}, and a
raw non-ASCII byte equals its \verb{\%XX} escape), with no \code{*} or \code{$} stripping.
Groups that name the same crawler are combined, a \verb{User-agent: *} Allow
never cancels a named crawler's Disallow, and a Disallow is reported only
when every crawler its group names has the identical Allow. Bing and
Yandex also let Allow win an identical-pattern tie. The check also runs
on a body cut at \code{max_bytes}: lines past the cut cannot revive the
Disallow.

For \code{duplicate_rule}, two rules are the same when type and canonical
value are byte-identical, as for \code{disallow_tied_by_allow}, so \verb{/x} and
\verb{/x*} are not duplicates. Groups that name the same crawler are combined,
\code{*} and named crawlers are kept apart, and a rule is reported only when
every crawler its group names already has it from an earlier line. Empty
values and patterns that never match are skipped, and a body cut at
\code{max_bytes} is checked too.

A document holding a NUL byte gets none of \code{wildcard_rules_not_inherited},
\code{disallow_tied_by_allow} or \code{duplicate_rule}, because the parser stops a
value at the NUL while the matchers drop it and read on.
